{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310841,"nonce":"6juXXPaIQvQ9NBSg1fxn5S+4x54=","title":"First end-to-end bench of nue/llama-chat vs llama.cpp on Llama 3.2 1B Instruct Q4_K_M / Apple M1 Pro. Rayzor is correct (output matches ...","message":"- Hardware: Apple M1 Pro (10 cores: 8 perf + 2 eff, 16 GB unified memory)\n- Model: Llama 3.2 1B Instruct Q4_K_M GGUF (770 MB on disk)\n- Prompt: \"What is the capital of France?\"\n- Max tokens: 24\n- Rayzor: `--temperature 0.01` (near-greedy via inline TopK=50 LocalTempSampler)\n- llama.cpp: `--temp 0 --seed 42 --jinja`\n\n\n| Implementation     | Wall    | Tok/s   | Peak RSS  | Peak %CPU | Mean %CPU |\n|--------------------|---------|---------|-----------|-----------|-----------|\n| **rayzor (CPU)**   | 38.02 s | 0.22    | 3.9 GB    | 100%      | 98%       |\n| llama.cpp (Metal)  | 1.66 s  | 135.81  | 4.9 GB    | 83%       | 59%       |\n| llama.cpp (CPU)    | 3.13 s  | 72.40   | 5.7 GB    | 564%      | 188%      |\n\n- Rayzor is ~325× slower than llama.cpp CPU and ~617× slower than\n  llama.cpp Metal.\n- Rayzor uses only 1 core (peak 100%, mean 98%). llama.cpp CPU uses\n  ~5–6 cores (peak 564%, mean 188% ≈ 2 cores fully loaded on\n  average; the bursts are matmul tile compute).\n- Memory: Rayzor is smaller (3.9 GB vs 4.9–5.7 GB) because llama.cpp\n  pre-allocates the full 131072-token KV cache at startup. Rayzor's\n  KV cache scales with the configured context (4096 by default).\n- 770 MB Q4_K_M on disk → Rayzor expands ~5× to 3.9 GB resident\n  (mostly the dequant-to-F32 weights). llama.cpp keeps weights\n  quantised on GPU/CPU and only the KV cache is F16 → larger total\n  because of cache pre-alloc but smaller weight footprint.\n\n\n1. **Single-threaded.** `tensor_simd::axpy_slice`-driven matmul\n   currently runs in one thread. `rayzor.concurrent.NumaPool`\n   already exists (see [[project-numa-followups]] / Phase 1c of the\n   roadmap) but isn't wired into the matmul outer loop yet.\n\n2. **Dequant-then-matmul.** `Linear.fromQuant` stores the Q4_K_M\n   QTensor and dispatches to `QTensor.matmulXTQ` which decodes super-\n   blocks on the fly — better than the early dequant path but still\n   running a generic scalar inner loop. A SIMD-tiled Q4_K_M kernel\n   that does block-quant-aware mma would close most of the CPU gap.\n\n3. **No GPU dispatch.** Llama is currently the all-CPU path. The\n   existing GPU stack (wgpu/Metal compute, kernel cache) could\n   absorb the attention bmm + projection matmuls — Phase 1a's\n   device-aware Tensor would carry it.\n\n4. **Inference loop**: `model.forwardIds([nextId])` for every\n   decode step re-runs the full Module-graph dispatch chain. A\n   tighter prefill / decode split could share more work — but\n   that's optimisation polish, not a fundamental gap.\n\n\n```\ntools/llama-diff/bench.sh \"\u003cprompt\u003e\" [n_tokens]\n```\n\n- Wraps each binary in `/usr/bin/time -l` for end-of-run totals\n  (peak RSS, user/sys CPU time).\n- Concurrent `pgrep -P` sampler @ 5 Hz captures peak / mean\n  instantaneous %CPU.\n- Default: rayzor + llama.cpp Metal + llama.cpp CPU. `ONLY=rayzor`\n  / `llama-metal` / `llama` skip the others.\n\n\n- Wire NumaPool into the matmul outer loop (Phase 1c, queued).\n- SIMD-tiled Q4_K_M direct matmul (Phase 4 bonus).\n- GPU dispatch path through device-aware Tensor (Phase 1a).\n- Repeat bench at 64 / 256 token N to characterise prefill vs decode\n  separately — the current numbers blur them.\n\n\n- [[bugs-rope-interleaved-for-gguf]] — the correctness fix that made\n  this bench meaningful.\n- [[project-numa-followups]] — the compiler-side work needed before\n  NumaPool can dispatch into matmul cleanly.\n- [[project-wasm-roadmap]] / Phase 1c — the original \"NumaPool into\n  matmul\" task.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_llama_chat_first_bench` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}